# Real Estate Market Analyzer & Prediction Tool
**UTD UTDesign - Group 13**

## Project Overview
This project develops a customized real estate market analyzer equipped with an interactive graphical user interface (GUI). It addresses the lack of high-performing, accessible tools for everyday buyers and sellers by decoding complex property trends and forecasting market fluctuations.

## Core Architecture
We utilize a hybrid machine learning approach to capture both immediate property value and future market momentum:
* **Micro-Pricing (XGBoost):** Predicts current fair market values of specific properties based on cross-sectional physical characteristics (square footage, age) and hyper-local geography.
* **Macro-Forecasting (LSTM):** Forecasts broader market and neighborhood price trajectories (6–12 months out) using historical time-series data and dynamic economic indicators (interest rates, market sentiment).

## Data Sources
* **Redfin DFW Dataset:** Provides static micro-data (beds, baths, square footage, coordinates).
* **Zillow Economics Data (ZHVI):** Provides historical baseline appreciation trends.
* **Dynamic APIs:** FRED (Federal Reserve Economic Data) for live interest rates, and local APIs for live sentiment and neighborhood statistics.

## Project Structure
* `data/` - Raw and processed datasets (Ignored by Git)
* `notebooks/` - Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) and initial pipeline testing
* `src/` - Core Python scripts for data processing, feature engineering, and model training
* `models/` - Saved model weights (Ignored by Git)

## Setup and Installation
1. Clone the repository:
   ```bash
   git clone [https://github.com/husseinalkhafaji02/UTD-Design-Group-13.git](https://github.com/husseinalkhafaji02/UTD-Design-Group-13.git)

## Project Structure (expanded)

- `data/` - Raw and processed datasets (ignored by Git; large files excluded)
- `notebooks/` - Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) and experiments
- `src/` - Core Python scripts for data processing, feature engineering, and model training
   - `src/external_features_manager.py` - builds cached `data/zip_crime_lookup.csv` and `data/sentiment_time_series.csv`
   - `src/external_apis/` - API adapters for RapidAPI and Finnhub
- `models/` - Saved model weights (tracked per project decision)

## Environment & API keys

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in your keys (do NOT commit `.env`). The manager uses these variables:

```text
RAPIDAPI_KEY=your_rapidapi_key
RAPIDAPI_HOST=provider_host
RAPIDAPI_CRIME_URL=https://api.example.com/crime/{zip}
FINNHUB_KEY=your_finnhub_key
FINNHUB_SYMBOL=TESTSYM
OVERPASS_API_URL=https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter  # optional override
GDELT_API_URL=https://api.gdeltproject.org/api/v2/search/tv  # optional override
```

## Running the external features manager

After creating a `.env` file (or setting the environment variables), run:

```powershell
python src/external_features_manager.py
```

This produces:

- `data/zip_crime_lookup.csv` — Crime index per ZIP (used by XGBoost)
- `data/sentiment_time_series.csv` — Weekly sentiment time series (used by LSTM)

The manager prefers RapidAPI (crime) and Finnhub (sentiment) when keys are present; otherwise it falls back to Overpass/GDELT and will write neutral defaults if APIs are unavailable.

## Tests

Install `pytest` in your venv and run tests:

```powershell
python -m pip install pytest
python -m pytest -q
```

## Notes on secrets and git

The repository `.gitignore` now excludes common secret files such as `.env` and `keys.json`. Never commit your API keys.
